::: {.callout-note}
Welcome, students! Cambridge International AS & A Level Mathematics (9709) is a flexible modular curriculum. K-Education Hub provides a structured library of core study notes, topical worksheets, and actual exam past papers organized by individual papers to help you optimize your learning and achieve an A* grade.
:::

## 1. Course Introduction

<p>Cambridge International AS & A Level Mathematics (9709) provides students with a detailed understanding of mathematical principles, logical reasoning, and analytical techniques. The curriculum enables candidates to specialize in Pure Mathematics, Mechanics, and Probability & Statistics.</p>

### Assessment Outline

<p>Candidates can take the qualification either as an AS Level only or as a full A Level. To obtain the full A-Level Mathematics qualification, candidates must sit 4 papers in total, combining compulsory Pure Mathematics modules with application modules.</p>

<div class="table-wrapper" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
  <table class="premium-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Exam Paper</th>
        <th>Syllabus Component</th>
        <th>Format & Duration</th>
        <th>Marks</th>
        <th>AS-Level Weighting</th>
        <th style="text-align: right;">A-Level Weighting</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Paper 1</strong> (Pure Mathematics 1)</td>
        <td>Quadratics, functions, coordinate geometry, circular measure, trig, series, calculus</td>
        <td>Written exam &mdash; 1 hour 50 minutes</td>
        <td>75 marks</td>
        <td><strong>60%</strong></td>
        <td style="text-align: right;"><strong>30%</strong></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Paper 2</strong> (Pure Mathematics 2)</td>
        <td>Algebra, logarithmic/exponential functions, trig, differentiation, integration, numerical solutions</td>
        <td>Written exam &mdash; 1 hour 15 minutes</td>
        <td>50 marks</td>
        <td><strong>40%</strong></td>
        <td style="text-align: right;"><em>N/A (AS only)</em></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Paper 3</strong> (Pure Mathematics 3)</td>
        <td>Algebra, logs/exponentials, trig, differentiation, integration, numerical solutions, vectors, differential equations, complex numbers</td>
        <td>Written exam &mdash; 1 hour 50 minutes</td>
        <td>75 marks</td>
        <td>&mdash;</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;"><strong>30%</strong></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Paper 4</strong> (Mechanics)</td>
        <td>Forces and equilibrium, kinematics of straight-line motion, momentum, Newton's laws, energy, work and power</td>
        <td>Written exam &mdash; 1 hour 15 minutes</td>
        <td>50 marks</td>
        <td><strong>40%</strong></td>
        <td style="text-align: right;"><strong>20%</strong></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Paper 5</strong> (Probability & Statistics 1)</td>
        <td>Representation of data, permutations and combinations, probability, discrete random variables, Normal distribution</td>
        <td>Written exam &mdash; 1 hour 15 minutes</td>
        <td>50 marks</td>
        <td><strong>40%</strong></td>
        <td style="text-align: right;"><strong>20%</strong></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Paper 6</strong> (Probability & Statistics 2)</td>
        <td>Poisson distribution, linear combinations, continuous random variables, sampling/estimation, hypothesis tests</td>
        <td>Written exam &mdash; 1 hour 15 minutes</td>
        <td>50 marks</td>
        <td>&mdash;</td>
        <td style="text-align: right;"><strong>20%</strong></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

### A-Level Assessment Routes

<ul>
  <li><strong>AS Level Award</strong>: Typically taken in the first year, combining 2 papers (Paper 1 + one of Paper 2, 4, or 5).</li>
  <li><strong>Full A-Level Award</strong>: Compiled from 4 papers. The three standard routes are:
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Route 1</strong>: Paper 1, Paper 3, Paper 4 (Mechanics), and Paper 5 (Probability & Statistics 1). <em>(Most common)</em></li>
      <li><strong>Route 2</strong>: Paper 1, Paper 3, Paper 5 (Probability & Statistics 1), and Paper 6 (Probability & Statistics 2).</li>
      <li><strong>Route 3</strong>: Paper 1, Paper 3, Paper 4 (Mechanics), and Paper 6 (Probability & Statistics 2). <em>(Requires Route 1 / Mechanics knowledge)</em></li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>

### Syllabus Structure

<p>The core papers of the CIE A-Level Mathematics curriculum comprise the following detailed topics:</p>

<div class="syllabus-section">
::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Paper 1: Pure Mathematics 1


1.1 Quadratics<br/>
1.2 Functions<br/>
1.3 Coordinate geometry<br/>
1.4 Circular measure<br/>
1.5 Trigonometry<br/>
1.6 Series<br/>
1.7 Differentiation<br/>
1.8 Integration<br/>


:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Paper 2: Pure Mathematics 2


2.1 Algebra<br/>
2.2 Logarithmic and exponential functions<br/>
2.3 Trigonometry<br/>
2.4 Differentiation<br/>
2.5 Integration<br/>
2.6 Numerical solution of equations<br/>


:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Paper 3: Pure Mathematics 3


3.1 Algebra<br/>
3.2 Logarithmic and exponential functions<br/>
3.3 Trigonometry<br/>
3.4 Differentiation<br/>
3.5 Integration<br/>
3.6 Numerical solution of equations<br/>
3.7 Vectors<br/>
3.8 Differential equations<br/>
3.9 Complex numbers<br/>


:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Paper 4: Mechanics


4.1 Forces and equilibrium<br/>
4.2 Kinematics of motion in a straight line<br/>
4.3 Momentum<br/>
4.4 Newton's laws of motion<br/>
4.5 Energy, work and power<br/>


:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Paper 5: Probability & Statistics 1


5.1 Representation of data<br/>
5.2 Permutations and combinations<br/>
5.3 Probability<br/>
5.4 Discrete random variables<br/>
5.5 The normal distribution<br/>


:::

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Paper 6: Probability & Statistics 2


6.1 The Poisson distribution<br/>
6.2 Linear combinations of random variables<br/>
6.3 Continuous random variables<br/>
6.4 Sampling and estimation<br/>
6.5 Hypothesis tests<br/>
:::

</div>

